Subject: register VM recursion depth matches the stack VM's

What this does:
  Two changes, one per register VM build. Before them, a plain
  `function f(){ f(); }` overflowed after 192 frames in the monolith and 157
  in the threaded+record build, where the stack VM reaches 1393.
  Octane EarleyBoyer threw `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded` in
  both builds.

  Monolith: every JS-to-JS call re-enters `js_regvm_execute`, and its C frame
  was 5032 bytes. 4096 of those bytes came from one cold handler,
  `ROP_array_from`, which built its elements in a local `JSValue vals[256]`.
  That handler (and its `regvm_exec_one` arm and its generated step twin) now
  calls `regvm_array_from_regs`, which dups the registers straight into the
  new array's storage. The frame drops to about 1240 bytes and depth goes
  192 -> 770.

  Threaded+record: calls stay inside one C activation, but each record is
  pushed onto `rt->regvm_frames`. That arena started at 64 KB and could only
  grow while it was empty, because JSVarRefs point into live frames and a
  frame must never move. So recursion stopped at about 64 KB of records:
  157 frames. The arena is now a chain of chunks. A full chunk is never
  reallocated under live frames; the next frame starts a new chunk linked
  after it, popping back steps to the previous chunk, and one vacated chunk
  is kept as a spare so a recursion hovering at a boundary does not allocate
  each time. The limit is a byte budget equal to `rt->stack_size`
  (`JS_SetMaxStackSize`, 1 MB by default; 1 GB guard when unlimited), and
  hitting it is the same stack-overflow RangeError as before.
  `regvm_rec_of_sf` walks the chain for its membership test. Depth goes
  157 -> 2512.

  Recursion depth before the RangeError (o = before, p = after):

    test                       stack VM   mono o/p    thr+rec o/p
    f(){ f() }                 1393       192 / 770   157 / 2512
    method this.m()            1364       191 / 752   146 / 2336
    3 args                     1336       189 / 719   128 / 2048
    via [0].map(m)              819       175 / 550   164 / 455
    getter                     1056       178 / 589   169 / 499
    new C()                    1212       188 / 703   177 / 579

  Paths that recurse through native code (map, getters, constructors) still
  pay a `js_regvm_execute` frame each time, so they remain below the stack
  VM there. Shrinking that frame further is the follow-up.

  Octane EarleyBoyer (corpus/earleyboyer.js): stack VM 2711; monolith and
  threaded+record were RangeError, now 2368 and 2657.
  box2d checksum 546.1008391043414 in both builds; instructions:u
  thr+rec 25.92G -> 25.81G, cycles 12.26G -> 11.97G; monolith 37.27G ->
  37.27G, cycles 16.06G -> 15.98G. test262 50/81192 errors in both builds
  (unchanged).

Why we need it:
  Real programs (EarleyBoyer, deep recursive parsers and tree walkers)
  overflowed at a depth the stack VM handles easily. The guard was correct;
  the frames were too large.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Runtime-only.

Notes:
  `quickjs-regvm-step.inc` is regenerated by `.local/regvm-build/emit-step.py`
  from the fixed `quickjs.c`; its hunk is that regeneration.

